(KD) L38:     2. cite28†Memorization L39:     3. cite29†Memorization & Privacy in Knowledge Distillation (KD) L40:   9. cite30†8 Conclusion L41:   10. cite31†9 Acknowledgements L42:   11. cite32†References L43:   12. cite33†A Additional Experimental Results L44:     1. cite34†A.1 Easy-to-Memorize Examples on Additional Models and Datasets L45:     2. cite35†A.2 Extended Generalization Results L46:     3. cite36†A.3 Extended Analysis: Other Models and Datasets L47:       1. cite37†A.3.1 Memorization Overlap Analysis L48:       2. cite38†A.3.2 Feature Distributions Distinguishing Memorized vs. Non-Memorized Examples L49:     4. cite39†A.4 More Details On Our Memorization Classifier L50:     5. cite40†A.5 Dissecting Student and Baseline Memorization L51:     6. cite41†A.6 Evaluating Memorization During Distillation in a Pre-training Setup L52: cite42†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L53: \nL54: arXiv:2601.15394v1 [cs.CL] 21 Jan 2026\nL55: \nL56: \\addtolist\nL57: \nL58: [4]Northeastern University\\affiliationformat\nL59: # Memorization Dynamics in Knowledge Distillation for Language Models\nL60: Jaydeep Borkar Affiliation: Meta Superintelligence Labs Work done at Meta    Karan Chadha Affiliation: Meta Central Applied Science Work done at Meta    Niloofar Mireshghallah Affiliation: FAIR at Meta, Affiliation: Carnegie Mellon University Work done at Meta    Yuchen Zhang Affiliation: Meta Superintelligence Labs Work done at Meta    Irina-Elena Veliche Affiliation: Meta Superintelligence Labs    Archi Mitra Affiliation: Meta Superintelligence Labs    David A.\nL61: Smith    Zheng Xu Affiliation: Meta Superintelligence Labs    Diego Garcia-Olano Affiliation: Meta Superintelligence Labs\nL62: ###### Abstract\nL63: Knowledge Distillation (KD) is increasingly adopted to transfer capabilities from large language models to smaller ones, offering significant improvements in efficiency and utility while often surpassing standard fine-tuning. Beyond performance, KD is also explored as a privacy-preserving mechanism to mitigate the risk of training data leakage.\nL64: While training data memorization has been extensively studied in standard pre-training and fine-tuning settings, its dynamics in a knowledge distillation setup remain poorly understood. In this work, we study memorization across the KD pipeline using three large language model (LLM) families (Pythia, OLMo-2, Qwen-3) and three datasets (FineWeb, Wikitext, Nemotron-CC-v2).\nL65: We find: (1) distilled models memorize significantly less training data than standard fine-tuning (reducing memorization by more than 50%); (2) some examples are inherently easier to memorize and account for a large fraction of memorization during distillation (over  95%); (3) student memorization is predictable prior to distillation using features based on zlib entropy, KL divergence, and perplexity; and (4) while soft and hard distillation have similar overall memorization rates, hard distillation poses a greater risk: it inherits $2.7\\times$ more teacher-specific examples than soft distillation.\nL66: Overall, we demonstrate that distillation can provide both improved generalization and reduced memorization risks compared to standard fine-tuning.\nL67: ^{†}^{†}date: August 24, 2026^{†}^{†}correspondence: jaijborkar@gmail.com & diegoolano@meta.com cite43†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Experimental framework. (Left) Training setup: The Teacher and Baseline models are independently fine-tuned from Pythia 12B and Pythia 1.4B base models, respectively, on dataset $\\mathcal{D}$ using cross-entropy.\nL68: The Student is initialized from the Pythia 1.4B base model and distilled on the same dataset $\\mathcal{D}$ to match the Teacher’s logit distribution by minimizing KL divergence. (Right) Discoverable memorization evaluation: We prompt models with 50-token prefixes from training examples. An example is classified as memorized if the model’s greedy generation of the subsequent 50 tokens exactly matches the ground truth suffix.\nL69: ## 1 Introduction\nL70: Knowledge distillation (KD) (cite44†Hinton et al., 2015 ), which transfers knowledge from larger teacher models to smaller student models, has been widely adopted in large language model (LLM) training due to its benefits in utility, efficiency and privacy (cite45†Agarwal et al., 2023 ; cite46†Ko et al., ). KD is used to improve model quality by distilling knowledge from more powerful models, such as the DeepSeek-R1 distilled series (cite47†Guo et al., 2025 ) and the Gemma distilled models (cite48†Gemma et al., 2024 ).\nL71: Since training language models with billions of parameters from scratch is computationally expensive (cite49†Hoffmann et al., 2022 ), KD has emerged as an effective approach to obtain higher-quality models with comparable or even reduced compute.\nL72: Beyond utility and efficiency, distillation is frequently cited as a potential defense against privacy vulnerabilities, specifically to protect the teacher’s training data against privacy attacks (cite50†Papernot et al., 2018 ; cite51†Shejwalkar and Houmansadr, 2021 ; cite52†Tang et al., 2022 ). One such class of attacks is data extraction attacks, where an attacker can extract some portions of the training data from LLMs (cite53†Carlini et al., 2019 ; cite54†Carlini et al., 2020 ; cite55†Nasr et al., 2023 ).\nL73: While memorization and training data extraction have been extensively studied in standard pre-training (cite56†Carlini et al., 2023 ; cite57†Biderman et al., 2023a ; cite58†Prashanth et al., 2025 ; cite59†Morris et al., 2025 ; cite60†Wei et al., 2025 ) and fine-tuning (cite61†Mireshghallah et al., 2022 ; cite62†Borkar, 2023 ; cite63†Zeng et al., 2024 ; cite64†Borkar et al., 2025 ), the mechanics of memorization remain poorly understood in a traditional KD setup.\nL74: In such settings, the student is trained to mimic the teacher’s distribution using the Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence loss (cite65†Kullback and Leibler, 1951 ).\nL75: To address this gap, we systematically study training data memorization during knowledge distillation in a fine-tuning setup by examining both the amount and the characteristics of example sequences extracted from distilled models compared to standard fine-tuned baselines (trained with conventional cross-entropy loss).\nL76: We distill models from the Pythia (cite66†Biderman et al., 2023b ), OLMo-2 (cite67†OLMo et al., 2025 ), and Qwen-3 (cite68†Yang et al., 2025 ) families using three distinct datasets, FineWeb (cite69†Penedo et al., 2024 ), WikiText (cite70†Merity et al., 2016 ), and the synthetic Nemotron-CC-v2 dataset (cite71†et al., 2025 ). We report the following four main findings:\nL77:   1. 1.\nL78: \nL79: Logit-level distillation using KL divergence significantly reduces memorization of training data and yields better generalization compared to standard fine-tuning. Crucially, the student partially inherits the teacher’s generalization capabilities while inheriting only 0.9% of its memorization (Section cite11†3.1 ).\nL80: \nL81:   2. 2.\nL82: Certain examples are consistently memorized across models of varying sizes within the same family because they are inherently easier to memorize. Furthermore, we find that distilled models preferentially memorize these easy-to-memorize sequences (Section cite15†3.2 ).\nL83: \nL84:   3. 3.\nL85: Memorization in student models is predictable before distillation (Section cite18†4 ). Furthermore, we investigate the mechanism behind distillation’s regularizing effect by analyzing sequence-level Shannon entropy and log probability (Figure cite72†11 & Section cite21†5 ).\nL86: \nL87:   4. 4.\nL88: Logit-level (soft) and sequence-level (hard) distillation show significant memorization overlap, with the soft-distilled student capturing over 70% of the examples memorized by the hard-distilled student. Despite this similarity, hard distillation poses a greater risk of inheriting memorization of difficult examples from the Teacher (2.7$\\times$ more compared to soft distillation) (Section cite22†6 ).\nL89: ## 2 Background and Experimental Setup\nL90: Our goal is to study memorization and extraction of training data within a knowledge distillation framework.\nL91: We adopt the following definitions throughout the paper: (1) Teacher model ($M_{teacher}$): the larger model that serves as a guide for training the smaller model; (2) Student model ($M_{student}$): the smaller model trained to mimic the teacher; (3) Baseline model ($M_{baseline}$): a model of the same size as the distilled student, but fine-tuned independently using standard cross-entropy loss; and (4) Dataset $\\mathcal{D}$: the specific dataset used to train all three models.\nL92: We now detail our experimental framework, comprising the knowledge distillation setup and our memorization evaluation metrics, as illustrated in Figure cite73†1 . Section cite26†7 discusses prior work on memorization and privacy in knowledge distillation.\nL93: ##### Knowledge Distillation (KD)\nL94: \nL95: Our main experiments use the Pythia models (cite66†Biderman et al., 2023b ) and the FineWeb dataset (cite69†Penedo et al., 2024 ). To ensure the robustness of our findings, we extend our analysis to the OLMo-2 (cite67†OLMo et al., 2025 ) and Qwen-3 (cite68†Yang et al., 2025 ) families, as well as the Nemotron-CC-v2 (cite71†et al., 2025 ) and WikiText-103 (cite70†Merity et al., 2016 ) datasets.\nL96: For the primary setup, we use $1\\text{M}$ examples with a sequence length of 256 tokens from the July 2025 Common Crawl dump of FineWeb as our dataset $\\mathcal{D}$. We first fine-tune the Pythia 12B base model on $\\mathcal{D}$ using cross-entropy loss to obtain the teacher model $M_{teacher}$.\nL97: To obtain the student $M_{student}$, we train the Pythia 1.4B base model on $\\mathcal{D}$ using the teacher’s guidance via forward KL divergence loss (cite65†Kullback and Leibler, 1951 ; cite44†Hinton et al., 2015 ; cite46†Ko et al., ), defined as:\nL98:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{KD}}=T^{2}\\sum_{i=1}^{|\\mathcal{V}|}P_{\\text{teacher}}^{\\tau}(i)\\log\\frac{P_{\\text{teacher}}^{\\tau}(i)}{P_{\\text{student}}^{\\tau}(i)},$$  |  | (1)\nL99: \nL100: where $T$ is the temperature parameter (set to $2.0$ in our experiments), and\nL101: \nL102:  | $$P^{\\tau}(i)=\\mathrm{softmax}\\!\\left(\\frac{z_{i}}{T}\\right)=\\frac{\\exp(z_{i}/T)}{\\sum_{j=1}^{|\\mathcal{V}|}\\exp(z_{j}/T)}$$  |  | (2)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMartingale Foresight Sampling: A Principled Approach to Inference-Time LLM Decoding (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.15482v1)\nciteturn27732view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.15482v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 391\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMARS: Unleashing the Power of Speculative Decodingvia Margin-Aware Verification (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.15498v1)\nciteturn27732view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.15498v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 356\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nParallelism and Generation Order in Masked Diffusion Language Models: Limits Today, Potential Tomorrow (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.15593v1)\nciteturn27732view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.15593v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 733\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhen Sharpening Becomes Collapse: Sampling Bias and Semantic Coupling in RL with Veri